Let the daemon veto a stale-busy verdict, not only a free one

Layer 2 of #159. The queue already owned a daemon-truth check and never ran it
where it mattered: `checkMemoryAdmission` sat behind `slotFree`, so a phantom
slot failed the slot test first and the probe never fired. It could only ever
catch under-counting drift — the wrong direction, since over-counting is what
stops work starting. The `pickup-wedged` card computed exactly this comparison
and only ever reported it.

Now a *blocked* pickup is the verdict that gets double-checked, and a released
slot is re-tested before the cycle gives up. Two halves, both one-directional
and fail-safe, because freeing a slot out from under live work is worse than
the wedge it fixes:

- a session entry goes only when the daemon positively answers 404. That needs
  existence, not liveness, so it cannot reuse `isContainerRunning`: an entry in
  `setup` is created but not started, and a parked pass is `docker stop`ped
  since #93, and that predicate calls both of them gone. Hence
  `containerIsAbsent`, fail-safe in the same direction as its sibling — a
  daemon that errors or times out says nothing, and nothing is what it decides
  (#152's discipline). Parked entries are skipped outright: no slot to reclaim,
  so no reason to ask.
- a reservation goes only once it has outlived a 20-minute provisioning grace
  *and* its task still has no container recorded. `startTask` writes the
  container id as soon as it exists, so its absence after the grace means
  provisioning is never going to finish. No Docker call for this half.
  `slotReservations` becomes a Map carrying the reservation time to make that
  judgement possible.

The grace is generous on purpose: a cold `ensureImage` build runs inside
`createWorkspaceContainer`, which is the same worst case that sizes the
watchdog's OCCUPANCY_DIVERGED_MINUTES. A test pins that a reservation inside
the grace is left alone.

The reconciliation runs every ~30s rather than every poll: on a one-slot box
"busy" is the normal reading, so per-poll probing would ask Docker about every
occupant every 2s forever, and the wedges this clears lasted 20 minutes and
more. It is bounded by DOCKER_PROBE_TIMEOUT_MS per call, because it sits in the
dispatch path — the one place #115 and #128 say must never stall.

Confirmed red-capable: all three new tests fail with the reconciliation call
disabled.

